```python
from collections import deque

def max_sliding_window(arr, k):
    dq = deque()  # stores indices
    result = []
    for i in range(len(arr)):
        while dq and arr[dq[-1]] <= arr[i]:
            dq.pop()
        dq.append(i)
        if dq[0] <= i - k:
            dq.popleft()
        if i >= k - 1:
            result.append(arr[dq[0]])
    return result
```

Three things happen per index: pop smaller-or-equal trailing values from the back, push the current index, then expire the front if it's fallen out of the window (`dq[0] <= i - k`). Once the window has reached full width (`i >= k - 1`), the front of the deque is the answer for that window. Each index is pushed exactly once and popped at most once across the whole run, giving O(n) total time.
